- DESIGN (a) chosen. ONE op: kernel-uefi-alloc-region base slot allocate-type memory-type page-count address-hint -> the base of the pages, or 0. THE ARGUMENT: roots are NOT addresses the compiler knows (kernel-boot-info is a word the firmware handed over) — a root is an address SOMETHING OUTSIDE THE EXPRESSION IS ANSWERABLE FOR: the entry contract, the packager, and now the UEFI spec. What makes the third usable rather than quotable is that **the head IS the allocation**: the out-word AllocatePages writes through is at [rsp+0x20] in the emitted frame, so the program cannot present an address to it
- WHY (b) IS WORSE, stated precisely: kernel-adopt-region is not an extension of the rule but its deletion with a log file — the length becomes an assertion by the author about a number the author also computed, and once it exists it is the cheapest writable base (GetMemoryMap's buffer, HandleProtocol's interface, LoadedImage->ImageBase are all one adoption away), so within one loader every window is rooted at an adoption and the report lists them all and means nothing
- Encoding: lea r9,[rsp+0x20] / call r11 / mov r10,[rsp+0x20] / xor r11,r11 (sets flags, so it PRECEDES) / test rax,rax / cmovne r10,r11. Zero-on-failure because a null base is what every bounded op already refuses; **cmove is one bit away and inverts the operation with nothing faulting to say so** — pinned as an explicit `not`
